g edge, and a unanimous expert consensus of Safe-to-Likely R point to a comfortable Republican margin, with Polymarket pricing Democratic chances near 15%. A slight Democratic shift is warranted only for reports of Trump approval softening in red North Carolina districts, keeping this Likely rather than Safe R.", "watch": "Any credible general-election polling showing Knott under single digits, or a major outside-spending push for Barringer, would move this toward Tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Brad Knott (R) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term Republican Brad Knott defends a Raleigh-area seat with an R+8 partisan lean against Democrat Paul Barringer, a first-time candidate from Sanford. Knott has raised about $1.3 million and holds a cash advantage, while Barringer has kept pace financially with roughly $1 million raised but faces a difficult structural map. A Libertarian candidate also appears on the ballot.\n\nNo public general-election polls are available, so the picture rests on fundamentals and expert judgment. Nine raters unanimously place the race at Safe or Likely Republican, and prediction markets give Democrats only about a 15 percent chance. Headlines note some polling softness for Trump in red North Carolina districts, the main opening for Barringer, but nothing yet suggests the seat is in serious play.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0835, "margin": -8.64}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0743, "margin": -10.0}]}